Titre$ H Y P H O L O M A   -   NOORDELOS (2011)
Com$ Strophariaceae - Adaptée par Paul Bertéa 31-10-2013
1,2 $ On wood, often in large clusters
1,6 $ On other substrate (herbaceous plants, raw humus, peat, mosses, soil), single or in groups.
2,0 $ Stipe rooting in substrate, fibrillose-subsquamulose; smell strong, aromatic to nauseating $ H. radicosum
2,3 $ Stipe not rooting, innately fibrillose or with some loose fibrils; smell none or different
3,0 $ Stipe with girdles of while veil $ H. dispersum
3,4 $ Stipe without girdles of white veil
4,0 $ Lamellae grey when young; taste mild; on coniferous wood $ H. capnoides
4,5 $ Lamellae yellow or yellow-green when young; taste mild or very bitter; on deciduous and coniferous wood
5,0 $ Taste very bitter; pileus yellow with yellow-brown to orange-brown centre; on coniferous and deciduous wood $ H. fasciculare
5,0 $ Taste mild; pileus warm red-brown, often with whitish or pale yellow margin; on deciduous wood $ H. lateritium
6,0 $ Basidiocarps growing from dark brown, irregularly shaped sclerotia; usually on piles of woodchips $ H. tuberosum
6,7 $ Basidiocarps not originating from sclerotia, generally not on woodchips
7,8 $ Spore length < 10 µm
7,11 $ Spore length > 10 µm
8,9 $ Lamella edge with abundant chrysocystidia among cheilocystidia
8,10 $ Lamella edge without chrysocystidia
9,0 $ Lamella edge yellow; pileus greenish yellow or brown yellow with red-brown centre $ H. polytrichi
9,0 $ Lamella edge concolorous with sides or paler; pileus entirely red-brown when fresh, turning olive-buff when old $ H. fulvidulum
10,0 $ Pileus (sordid) red-brown, sometimes with yellow tinge; spores 7.0-9.0 (-9.5) x 4.5-5.5 µm $ H. subericaeum
10,0 $ Pileus olive-grey with red-brown centre; spores 8.5-11.0 (-11.5) x (4.5-) 5.0-5.5 (-6.0) µm $ H. olivaceotinctum
11,12 $ Spore length exceeding 12.5 µm
11,13 $ Spores length 10-12.5 µm
12,0 $ Pileus often translucently striate; spores 13-17 (-18) x 6.5-8.0 µm more or less amygdaliform in side-view, roughened under oil-immersion with small, but distinct germ pore $ H. udum
12,0 $ Pileus not translucently striate; spores 12.5-15.5 x 6.5-8.0 µm, ellipsoid to oblong in side- view, smooth under oil-immersion, with rather wide germ pore, which sometimes gives the spore a truncate appearance $ H. ericaeum
13,14 $ Spores relatively thick-walled with large germ pore, lamellae with distinct violaceous purple tinge when mature
13,15 $ Spores relatively thin-walled with small germ pore; lamellae lacking distinct violaceous purple tinges when mature (species complex of H. elongatum)
14,0 $ Lamellae with distinct yellow tinge when young; subpellis well-differentiated $ H. ericaeoides
14,0 $ Lamellae without yellow tinges when young; subpellis not differentiated $ H. ericaeum sensu Romagnesi
15,0 $ Pileus moderately dark to dark brown-olivaceous with more brown to orange brown centre; spores 8.5-11.0 (-11.5) x (4.5-) 5.0-5.5 (-6.0) µm $ H. olivaceotinctum
15,16 $ Pileus relatively pale yellowish at margin, more reddish brown at centre
16,0 $ Lamellae pale grey without distinct yellow tinges when young; chrysocystidia absent along lamella edge; spores narrow, oblong, 5.5-6.5 (-7.0) µm wide $ H. laeticolor
16,17 $ Lamellae with distinct yellow tinges when young; lamella edge with chrysocystidia; spores slightly broader, (6.0-) 6.5-8.0 µm wide
17,0 $ Spores amygdaliform in side-view $ H. xanthocephalum
17,0 $ Spores ellipsoid-oblong in side-view $ H. elongatum
